1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,252
Have you ever noticed a pricing

2
00:00:02,252 --> 00:00:06,175
page with an overpriced hundred-forty-nine-dollar tier

3
00:00:06,175 --> 00:00:07,991
that almost nobody needs?

4
00:00:07,991 --> 00:00:09,734
You are not meant to buy

5
00:00:09,734 --> 00:00:11,260
it - you are meant to

6
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:12,640
measure against it.

7
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,942
MIT economist Dan Ariely proved why

8
00:00:14,942 --> 00:00:16,652
in Predictably Irrational.

9
00:00:16,652 --> 00:00:18,626
When he placed a useless decoy

10
00:00:18,626 --> 00:00:21,257
option beside a premium magazine bundle,

11
00:00:21,257 --> 00:00:23,888
eighty-four percent of readers chose the

12
00:00:23,888 --> 00:00:24,874
expensive plan.

13
00:00:24,874 --> 00:00:26,058
Without the decoy,

14
00:00:26,058 --> 00:00:27,900
only thirty-two percent did.

15
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:30,430
Psychologists call this Price Anchoring.

16
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:33,656
Your brain struggles to calculate standalone value,

17
00:00:33,656 --> 00:00:35,554
so sellers plant a high anchor

18
00:00:35,554 --> 00:00:38,020
first to make seventy-nine dollars feel

19
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:38,400
cheap.

20
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:41,611
Use the Blank-Sheet Test before any purchase:

21
00:00:41,611 --> 00:00:43,323
cover the pricing table,

22
00:00:43,323 --> 00:00:45,749
write down what solving that exact

23
00:00:45,749 --> 00:00:47,461
problem is worth to you,

24
00:00:47,461 --> 00:00:49,530
and reject anything above it.

25
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:52,145
A steep discount off a fake

26
00:00:52,145 --> 00:00:54,858
anchor is still a real leak.

27
00:00:54,858 --> 00:00:56,601
Keep that in mind,

28
00:00:56,601 --> 00:00:57,570
because...
